--- body ---

## Context

Cycle 3 as pre-registered in the concept note (sect. 8) and the 1024
journal: the local flash-next family at EXPLICIT @medium vs the hosted
lineage twin.

## Arms

Revised 2026-10-12 by the author (supersedes the 2026-10-06 list; no leg
had run). Six local arms: four on Flash-Next, two on Mellum.

Scoring split (author, 2026-10-12). Cycle 3 informs a choice between two
deployments: A = Flash-Next on the 5070 Ti as orchestrator + Mellum on the
A4000 as local coder; B = Flash-Next on both GPUs (A4000 helper) + a hosted
coder. Reviewers in both: Flash-Next at lower thinking, Haiku, Luna. Same
weights give the same answers on either topology, so quality is scored once
per model and effort, and the topologies are compared on speed only.

- Full 10-ticket legs, panel-scored (the scatterplot points): f2x and f2m
  (Flash-Next, A4000 helper, xhigh / medium: orchestrator and lower-thinking
  reviewer; helper because it is faster, its quality stands for solo too),
  ml1 (Mellum, scored on the 5070 Ti for test time; same answers on the
  A4000, whose speed comes from qualification: the deployment-A coder), q8m and q8x (hosted
  qwen3.8-flash: a deployment-B coder candidate). Haiku, Luna and the other
  hosted coders come from cycle 2.
- Speed-only, 3 tickets (0470, 0532, 0452: author, 2026-10-12): f1x, f1m (Flash-Next, 5070 Ti solo). Their time and
  tokens go in the readout; they are not on the scatterplot.
- Mellum screen: ml1 runs the 3 speed-check tickets first; the remaining 7
  legs run only if it finishes them (no collapse on the large one). If it
  collapses, deployment A is out and ml1 stops at 3 legs.
- ml0 (Mellum on the A4000) is dropped (author, 2026-10-12: less test
  time).

Context rule (author, 2026-10-12): every local arm runs at ctx 131072. An
arm that cannot load 131072 on its card is dropped, not run shorter.

- Local Flash-Next IQ3_S (b2), Strata v0.1.43, CUDA 13.0, as a 2x2 (four
  arms, smoke each first): effort @medium | @xhigh, explicit (xhigh is the
  cycle-2 template default, so the @xhigh arms anchor against cycle 2);
  topology 5070 Ti solo | 5070 Ti + A4000 as expert helper. pi sends no
  effort to local servers (1024, 2026-10-05), so the level is set in each
  arm's Strata config, `"reasoning_effort": "medium"` or `"high"` (the config
  rejects the literal "xhigh"; high maps to xhigh) (#1641:
  the default for requests that carry none). Qualification checks that the
  two levels give different thinking lengths on the same prompt; if they do
  not, the 2x2 collapses to two arms. All four use a
  frozen expert profile (`--expert-profile`, see
  Strata settings below). Preload off is not a tournament arm (author,
  2026-10-12), and not a valid configuration either: v0.1.43 refuses to
  serve these packs without it (speculative verify "needs the profile-filled
  VRAM expert tier").
- Mellum 2.1 12B-A2.5B Thinking Q8_0 (JetBrains), llama-server, ctx 131072,
  `/data/models/gguf/mellum/`, on A4000 solo | 5070 Ti solo (two arms).
  No effort levels: its chat template only knows `enable_thinking` (on by
  default, no budget, no reasoning_effort). Identity: @thinking (template
  default); confirm from session evidence at the smoke. Sampling, set on
  llama-server for both arms: `--temp 0.6 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 20`, the
  model card's recommended setting (HF card, usage and GGUF sections).
  Caveat for the readout: the vendor's agentic scores (Pi v0.73.1) used
  "each model's default sampling (temperature 1.0 for Mellum2.1)", a 114K
  context and up to 16K tokens per turn, so our runs are not a replication
  of those scores.
- Hosted: qwen3.8-flash, lineage twin of flash-next (same architecture,
  NOT the same weights; label it "lineage twin" in every table), at
  @default(xhigh) and @medium, DIRECT DashScope via the custom pi provider
  (QWEN_API_KEY_AEDIST, dashscope-intl). Recheck the catalog at launch; if
  flash-next itself is served, take the true twin.
- Dropped: b3 Q2_0 and Coder IQ1_M (cycle 2 means 20.8 and 21.1 / 30 vs
  26.2 for b2). Gemma 4 26B-A4B cancelled by the author on 2026-10-12, after
  its qualification round (log entry of that date); NVFP4 weights deleted.

Panel: the cycle-2 panel (gemini / grok / minimax) stays. The gemini-seat
replacement decided earlier on 2026-10-12 lapsed with Gemma: no Google
candidate remains.

Mellum runs in its own window, not beside the Flash-Next arms
(author, 2026-10-12).

GPU placement is NOT an arena-level knob: each local arm's placement lives
in its launch script (`~/arena/start-arm.sh`, `~/Strata/strata-*.json`).
Before the first leg, every new arm pins its placement explicitly
(`CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID`, device list, CUDA 13.0 lib dirs) and the
pre-registration records it. Observed on padme 2026-10-12, still from the
pre-swap topology: `strata-iq3_s.json` lists `gpu: [0, 1]` with CUDA 12.8
lib dirs (nvidia-smi index 0 = A4000, 1 = 5070 Ti), already loads an
expert profile dated 2026-10-03, and arms c/c2 keep `--tensor-split 16,12`.

## Strata settings (reviewed 2026-10-12 against v0.1.43)

Engine: the arena must point at `~/Strata-daily` (v0.1.43, sm_86 + sm_120,
CUDA 13.0). `~/Strata/engine` is v0.1.38 built for sm_86 only.

Preload, two flags:
- `--expert-profile P` loads the VRAM expert tier from a profile at start.
- `--expert-profile-save P` (#477, `--serve`) writes what the adaptive tier
  learned, on quit and every `--expert-profile-save-every` minutes
  (default 10).
- The padme bench numbers above (solo 89/85/115, helper 100/92/119) were
  taken WITH `--expert-profile` (`Strata-daily/data/expert-profile.bin`,
  2026-10-11): the tournament arms reuse that condition.
- Preload off vs on: dropped, the engine does not serve without the
  profile. The bench with the frozen profile is the speed record.
- Profile protocol: build one profile per topology on a warm-up of
  non-tournament prompts, then freeze it. No `--expert-profile-save` during
  legs, so no leg learns from an earlier tournament leg. Mind trap #831
  (the profile's length caps the expert arena).

Other settings that change the comparison:
- Helper arm: the padme helper bench used `--expert-cache-device1` and
  `--remote-expert-opt` (#578, opt-in, rounds differently). Keep both and
  declare them in the arm identity.
- Not just topology: since v0.1.42 the route tail skip
  (`STRATA_ROUTE_TAIL_SKIP=7`) and the CPU-measured PCIe share are ON for one
  GPU and OFF with a helper or peer GPU. Solo vs helper compares two stacks,
  not one engine on two topologies; declare it, do not equalize.
- Since v0.1.41 the CPU takes part of short prompt chunks (<1,024 tokens, the
  typical tool result) on one NVIDIA GPU. The CPU pool already saturates the
  3945WX: run every Strata arm on the same CPU. If the 3975WX goes in, it goes
  in before the first Strata leg or after the last, never mid-cycle.
- Keep `--batch` / `"parallel"` off (the local lane is sequential) and pin
  `"gpu_order": "as_given"` on any layer split (v0.1.41 reorders by default).
- v0.1.43 ships `./bench.sh` (`tools/strata_bench.py`, 3-10 min): run it on
  each Strata arm after the smoke, as the speed record.
- Keep: no CUDA 13.2 (upstream #892), no `--prefill auto:32768` (#1468).

The engine duel (llama.cpp vs Strata on flash-next) needs a viable
llama.cpp GGUF (~110G Unsloth class): verify before declaring it, defer if
nothing good exists. The Hardin22 dual-GPU decode cluster is not merged
upstream: track it, do not wait for it.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Arms + hypotheses pre-registered before the first leg (concept note sect. 8)
- [ ] Smoke 0470 on every new arm before real legs
- [ ] Pass complete; paired multidimensional analysis (analyze2.py)
- [ ] skills/route grid.json gains or refreshes the local rows from cycle data (the grid also takes rows from the arena refresh, `scripts/tournament-analysis.py --grid`)

## Note (auteur, 2026-10-12)

Annotation seulement : le rerun reste à imaginer.

Le cycle 3 n'a pas démarré (aucun leg) : sa liste d'arms peut être réécrite
librement, à condition de l'inscrire dans la note conceptuelle §8 et le
journal 1024 avant le premier leg. L'auteur veut d'abord un cycle « codeur »
comparable au cycle 2, puis la refonte 1061.

Candidats nommés le 2026-10-12, encore à imaginer :
- Mellum 2.1 12B-A2.5B Thinking (JetBrains, sorti le 2026-10-08,
  Apache 2.0, famille distincte de Qwen, GGUF officiel). Carte du vendeur,
  auto-déclarée : LiveCodeBench v6 82,0, SWE-bench Verified 47,0,
  SWE-bench Pro 28,0, évals agentiques faites dans Pi v0.73.1. Q8_0
  téléchargé sur padme : `/data/models/gguf/mellum/`.
- Gemma 4 26B-A4B QAT UD-Q4_K_XL (Google), déjà sur padme.
- b2 (Flash-Next IQ3_S, Strata) @medium comme ancre. b3 et Coder sont
  jugés faibles au cycle 2 (moyennes 20,8 et 21,1 / 30, contre 26,2 pour
  b2), candidats à l'abandon.
- Le levier `--expert-profile-save` noté plus haut n'a pas encore été essayé.

Mesures padme des 2026-10-11/12 (memo padme `gpu-swap-5070ti-memo.md`,
PR padme #95–#97), pour le prix en temps des arms locaux :
- Flash-Next IQ3_S sur Strata v0.1.43, moteur CUDA 13.0 : A4000 en helper
  d'experts 100/92/119 tok/s (code/stats/writing), A4000 en peer
  95/90/126, 5070 Ti seul 89/85/115. Au départ (v0.1.38, A4000 + 3060) :
  40/39/48. Le moteur CUDA 12.8 plante dès que le 5070 Ti est la carte
  primaire (upstream #220/#224). Ne jamais utiliser CUDA 13.2 (upstream
  #892, sortie incohérente). Éviter `--prefill auto:32768` (upstream #1468).
- Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M sur llama-server, MTP, ctx 131072 : 35,5/31,9 tok/s
  avant le swap, 72,8/69,0 après (`--split-mode tensor --tensor-split 8,16`,
  `CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID`), 80,7 en prod.
- Reviewer sur l'A4000 pendant que Strata tourne seul sur le 5070 Ti :
  Mellum 2.1 Q8_0 charge 131K de contexte (13,8 Go), 108/110 tok/s seul,
  105/107 à côté de Strata. Gemma 4 26B-A4B charge 64K au plus (15,1 Go),
  85 tok/s seul, 81 à côté. Strata perd 3–5 % à côté. llama-server est
  épinglé sur les CPU 12–23 (Strata épingle son pool sur 0–11).
- Le pool CPU de Strata sature le 3945WX (12 threads, ~1 200 % CPU). Un
  3975WX est acheté ; le rerun CPU est prévu.
